ProjLib: stop the projection boundary's bisection when no parameter lies
between its two endpoints (mitcad#504).

DichExactBound brackets the end of a continuous normal projection between
a parameter that projects and one that does not. Its parameter tolerance
is obtained from the surface's UV tolerance divided by the projected
curve's derivative. The tolerance can be smaller than the spacing of the
curve's floating-point parameters. Once the bracket's endpoints are two
adjacent representable values, its midpoint rounds to one of them, so the
same point is projected again and the bracket never contracts.

Stop when the midpoint is an endpoint, keeping the last successfully
projected point: there is no closer parameter to test. The usual tolerance
test and the projection's success criteria are unchanged.

Upstream: new.
diff --git a/src/ModelingData/TKGeomBase/ProjLib/ProjLib_CompProjectedCurve.cxx b/src/ModelingData/TKGeomBase/ProjLib/ProjLib_CompProjectedCurve.cxx
--- a/src/ModelingData/TKGeomBase/ProjLib/ProjLib_CompProjectedCurve.cxx
+++ b/src/ModelingData/TKGeomBase/ProjLib/ProjLib_CompProjectedCurve.cxx
@@ -443,6 +443,12 @@ static void DichExactBound(gp_Pnt&                               Sol,
   while (fabs(Sol.X() - aNotSol) > Tol)
   {
     t = (Sol.X() + aNotSol) / 2;
+    // The UV-derived tolerance can be below the spacing of curve parameters.
+    // No representable point remains inside this bracket; keep its solution.
+    if (t == Sol.X() || t == aNotSol)
+    {
+      break;
+    }
     aPrjPS.Perform(t,
                    U0,
                    V0,
